{{also|infusé}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|ǵʰewd}}
From {{inh|en|enm|infusen}}, from {{der|en|la|infusus}}, from {{m|la|infundo}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfjuz/}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːz}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|infus}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to become an [[element]] of something; to [[insert]] or [[fill]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[steep]] in a liquid, so as to [[extract]] the soluble [[constituent]]s (usually [[medicinal]] or [[herbal]]).
#* {{rfdatek|en|Coxe}}
#*: One scruple of dried leaves is '''infused''' in ten ounces of warm water.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To inspire; to inspirit or animate; to fill (with).
#*{{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|V|iv|passage='''Infuse''' his breast with magnanimity.}}
#*{{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|III|ii|passage='''infusing''' him with self and vain conceit}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[instill]] as a quality.
#*{{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|IV|i|passage=That souls of animals '''infuse''' themselves / Into the trunks of men.}}
#* {{rfdatek|en|Jonathan Swift}}
#*: Why should he desire to have qualities '''infused''' into his son, which himself never possessed, or knew, or found the want of, in the acquisition of his wealth?
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To undergo [[infusion]].
#* ''Let it '''infuse''' for five minutes.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make an infusion with (an ingredient); to [[tincture]]; to [[saturate]].
#: {{rfquotek|en|Francis Bacon}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To pour in, as a liquid; to pour (into or upon); to shed.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Denham}}
#*: That strong Circean liquor cease to '''infuse'''.

